            5. The Details That Separate Good Pans From Expensive Ones
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Base flatness.</strong> The most important and least advertised property. A base that is flat, and stays flat through heat cycling, maintains full contact with the glass and therefore full coupling with the element. Cheap disc-bottom pans are the usual offenders, developing a slight convexity that makes them spin on the hob.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fully clad versus disc bottom.</strong> Cladding that runs up the walls gives even heat to the sides of the pan, which matters for sauces, risotto, and anything stirred. Disc bottoms cook well on the base and leave the walls comparatively cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Weight and thermal mass.</strong> Heavier pans hold temperature when cold food goes in, which is what produces a sear instead of steaming. Lighter pans respond faster to power changes, which suits delicate sauces. There is no universally correct answer — it is a genuine trade-off, and most kitchens want one of each.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Handle design and attachment.</strong> Riveted handles are strong and leave rivets inside the pan to clean around; welded handles leave a smooth interior and are typically found on more expensive cookware. Stay-cool handles work by being thin where they meet the pan, and are worth having on anything you lift often.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Lid fit.</strong> An underrated detail. A well-fitting lid holds moisture for braising and simmering, and a loose one lets a stew reduce when you wanted it to stay wet. Glass lids are convenient; metal lids tolerate higher oven temperatures.
          </p>
