        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cooking Technique That Induction Requires
          </h2>

          <p>
            Induction is powerful and immediate, and cooks coming from gas routinely burn their first few dinners by treating settings as equivalent. A medium setting on induction delivers considerably more heat into the pan than medium on a gas burner, and empty pans preheat alarmingly fast — a dry stainless skillet can reach smoking temperature in under a minute on high.
          </p>

          <p>
            Preheat properly, then adjust. For stainless, the water drop test is genuinely useful: flick a few drops into the pan, and when they form a single bead that skitters rather than spitting apart, the pan is at the right temperature for food to release cleanly. Most sticking on stainless is a preheating error rather than a cookware failure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect some noise. Induction produces a hum or buzz from the pan, particularly at high power and particularly with multi-ply construction, as the layers respond to the magnetic field. Heavier, better-made pans are generally quieter; a thin, lightweight pan can rattle audibly. It is normal and not a fault, though it surprises people on their first induction hob.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, lift rather than slide. Induction hobs are glass, and dragging cast iron or a gritty pan base across the surface scratches it permanently. Most surface damage on induction cooktops comes from sliding pans, not from heat or spills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Care That Keeps Cookware Working
          </h2>

          <p>
            For stainless, the main enemy is the discolouration people mistake for damage. A rainbow sheen is a harmless oxide layer, and white chalky spots are mineral deposits from hard water — both come off with a little vinegar or a stainless cleaner. Burnt-on food responds to simmering water with a spoonful of baking soda far better than to abrasion, and heavy scouring is what dulls a finish permanently.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cast iron, keep it dry and keep it used. Wash with hot water and a brush, dry it on the hob for a minute, and wipe a trace of oil over the surface while it is warm. That is the entire ritual, and it takes thirty seconds. Avoid soaking, avoid the dishwasher, and do not leave acidic sauces in it for hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            For enamelled cast iron, use wooden or silicone tools and avoid thermal shock — do not run cold water into a hot pot, which can craze the enamel. Nonstick needs the gentlest handling: medium heat at most, no aerosol cooking sprays, which build an invisible polymerised residue that ruins release, and hand washing regardless of what the dishwasher claim says.
          </p>

          <p>
            Across all of it, the biggest single favour you can do expensive cookware is to stop stacking it bare. A cheap felt pan protector between pieces prevents the scratching that makes a five-year-old pan look twenty, and on enamel it prevents chips that cannot be repaired.
          </p>
        </div>


