            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best induction cookware in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                All-Clad D3 tri-ply is our overall pick for everyday cooking — fully clad, reliably flat, responsive on induction, and warranted for life. Tramontina Tri-Ply Clad delivers nearly the same performance for about a third of the price. For a serious cook, Demeyere Atlantis is engineered specifically for induction and heats faster than anything else here.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if a pan works on induction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hold a fridge magnet against the base. If it grips firmly, the pan will work; weak attraction means poor coupling and slow heating; no attraction means the hob will not detect it at all. Clad stainless, cast iron, enamelled cast iron and carbon steel all work. Bare aluminium, copper and most glass or ceramic do not unless they have a bonded magnetic base plate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is expensive induction cookware worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the piece. An enamelled cast iron Dutch oven at $200 to $400 is a lifetime purchase and easy to justify. A mid-range fully clad skillet performs within a few percent of a premium one, so the extra money buys finish and warranty rather than cooking. Cast iron at $25 outperforms far more expensive pans at searing, and nonstick is a consumable that should never be bought as a set.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my pan buzz or hum on induction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The magnetic field causes slight vibration between the layers of a multi-ply pan, which you hear as a hum or buzz, usually loudest at high power. It is entirely normal and not a fault. Heavier, better-constructed pans tend to be quieter; thin lightweight pans can rattle noticeably. Making sure the pan sits flat and centred on the element usually reduces it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does cookware need to match my induction element size?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ideally, yes. The element heats the area of the pan directly above the coil, so a 12-inch pan on an 8-inch element has a cooler outer ring that cladding only partly evens out. Check your hob's element diameters before buying large skillets, and look for a bridge or flex zone if you regularly cook in oversized pans.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a cookware set or individual pieces?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Individual pieces, almost always. Sets inflate the piece count with sizes that stay in the cupboard, and four good pieces — a 12-inch skillet, a Dutch oven, a 3-quart saucepan, and one nonstick or cast iron pan — cover nearly all home cooking. The exception is a genuinely well-chosen value set such as Tramontina's, where the individual prices do not reward cherry-picking.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does cast iron scratch an induction cooktop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, if you slide it. The hob surface is glass, and dragging a rough cast iron base across it leaves permanent marks — the majority of induction surface damage comes from sliding rather than from heat. Lift pans on and off, keep the hob and the pan base clean of grit, and cast iron is otherwise one of the best performers on induction.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
