          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Induction Cookware Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most disappointment with induction traces back to the cookware rather than the hob, and usually to one of these five decisions.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Twelve-Piece Set</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sets pad the count with sizes nobody uses so the per-piece price looks good. Four well-chosen pieces bought individually cover almost all home cooking and will each be better than their equivalent in a budget set at the same total spend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Base Flatness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flatness determines contact area, and contact area determines how well the pan couples with the element. A cheap disc-bottom pan that develops a slight curve after a year heats slowly and unevenly, and no cooking technique fixes it. Fully clad construction holds flatness far better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Induction Settings Like Gas</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Medium on induction delivers considerably more heat than medium on a gas burner, and an empty pan can reach smoking temperature in under a minute. Start lower than you think for the first few weeks, and use the water drop test on stainless rather than guessing at preheat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sliding Pans Across the Hob</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Induction cooktops are glass and most of the scratches they accumulate come from dragging pans, especially cast iron with a rough base. Lift pans on and off, and keep both the surface and the pan base free of grit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Nonstick Set</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nonstick is a coating with a finite life, however it is marketed. One good nonstick skillet for eggs and fish is all any kitchen needs, replaced when it stops releasing — and buying three of them at once simply means all three wear out together.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
